hanges to content, evidence, conclusions, or author context. Each entry describes the page update. Reviews, approvals, editorial status, discussions, automated checks, and Git activity alone do not qualify. Discussion remains in its own section; internal audit records remain separate.</p></section><section><h2>Public attribution</h2><p>Accepted proposals and author context link to authenticated ORCID profiles. Pending, declined, withdrawn, or rejected work is visible only to its author and moderators. Accepted-but-unpublished proposals are labeled clearly until linked to an exact upstream Git revision.</p></section><section><h2>Messaging</h2><p>Contact is opt-in and mutual. Researchers can disable new requests, decline a request, block another account, or report a message. Participants see their conversation; moderators see reported material only as needed to review safety or conduct. Messaging is not end-to-end encrypted.</p></section><section><h2>Recognition and appeals</h2><p>Stars are quality milestones with public reasons, not a leaderboard. New awards at levels one through three require qualifying published scientific contributions. A paper counts once regardless of how many pages it improves; historical awards retain their recorded policy. Levels four and five require human review. Supporter status never affects recognition. Appeals go to <a href="mailto:researchers@wikibiome.com">researchers@wikibiome.com</a>.</p></section><section><h2>Conference and Roundtable selection</h2><p>The interest registry creates no event entitlement. Researchers with four or five stars, active interest, and separate early-invitation consent may enter a private shortlist for the event types they select. Human organizers assess subject fit, quality, conflicts, conduct, and interdisciplinary representation before any invitation.</p></section><section><h2>Editorial publication</h2><p>Claims, submissions, proposals, and discussions cannot edit the generated corpus directly. Accepted work becomes a change packet for the upstream repository and passes existing sync, audit, build, review, and publication gates.</p></section><section><h2>Related policies</h2><p><Link href="/privacy">Privacy</Link> · <Link href="/terms">Terms of Use</Link> · <Link href="/research-standard">WikiBiome-Ready Research</Link> · <Link href="/roundtable">Research event interest</Link></p></section></article></main></>; }
